  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}(2-x)({x}^{2}+3x+3)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
  "worked_solution": "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+3x+3=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(3)^{2}-4(1)(3)=-3<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=2.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}(2-0)({0}^{2}+3(0)+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)(3)}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,2),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14240558/assets/dac93a6d36f27b7c9eeb6640370d3736fa8353ee12697721a8ab1b416915dbef.png)",
